  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe northwest flow pattern aloft and stalled front to the\nsouthwest will continue to dictate the weather across the area\nthrough Thursday night. For today, a mid-level shortwave will\nripple through the northwest flow this afternoon. This will\nsupport a surface trough the will move through the eastern Great\nLakes and flare up showers and storms in Northeast Ohio and\nNorthwest PA this afternoon and evening. With better lift in\nthis area and some instability, some storms could be stronger\nwith gusty winds and large hail, but any severe event would be\nisolated. Further southwest, the shortwave will reinvigorate \nshowers and storms along the front in Indiana and Southwest \nOhio. Some of these could spread into Northwest and Central Ohio\nthis afternoon. Any afternoon and evening activity should\nlargely wipe out instability and any large development tonight,\nbut some stray showers may develop over and near the lake with\nsome isentropic lift over the area.\n\nFor Thursday into Thursday night, the front to the southwest \nwill try to lift north and get into Central Ohio, if not into \nthe southwest portions of the forecast area. One more shortwave \nwill ripple through the northwest flow aloft and allow for more \nrain and storms to develop. Believe that any storms with any \nbite to them will be southwest of the forecast area with the \nbetter instability. However, any storms will have heavy rain and\nthe potential for localized flooding does exist. High pressure\nwill build from the northwest for Friday, pushing the front and\nrain chances out of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe upper ridge over the Ohio and Tennessee Valleys will recede\nsouth a bit on Saturday into Sunday and allow for northwest flow\non the northeast side of the ridge. This will allow for a series\nof shortwaves to move through the region starting as early as\nSaturday afternoon with most impacts on Saturday night into\nSunday. These shortwaves will support shower and storm chances\nacross the area. There is a non-zero chance for strong to severe\nstorms on Sunday with a more organized surface low moving\nthrough the eastern Great Lakes region. Temperatures ahead of\nthe low and Sunday convection could get into the mid 80s.",
